          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em quantum group
            refers to various objects which are deformations of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+functions"} algebras of functions
            on)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+groups"} semisimple Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            %em deforming
            the function algebras on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+groups"} semisimple Lie groups
            or to the enveloping algebras of Kac-Moody Lie algebras.
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            It is a common experience in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            that a number of mathematical structures behaves very similarly to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie
            groups. After the impetus of the theory of quantum
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+systems"} integrable systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            and which deform enveloping algebras of (semisimple) Lie algebras, or algebras of functions on the corresponding algebraic groups. These deformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_q
            depend on a parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            (sometimes one prefers a formal parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            with
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q = e^{h}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            of the deformation is an
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            for these ‘quantum’ examples is highly developed; in fact many phenomena in the representation theory of semisimple Lie algebras (e.g. canonical bases) were discovered first as a limiting case of constructions in the quantum case, which become degenerate in the classical case (the principle that quantization removes degeneracy). While representations for generic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            parallel classical ones, the theory at roots of unity is peculiar and related to the representation theory of affine Lie algebras; the quantum groups at roots of unity as algebras have big centers.
          %p
            Nowadays, both the class of examples and the class of formalisms has been extended a lot, hence the term ‘quantum group’ is not a fixed notion but rather a collective term for a rather author-dependent class of group-like objects, most often subclasses or extensions of the concept of Hopf algebras which are sometimes required to belong to families of deformations of their classical counterparts. One of the common features is that if we forget the group-like features, the examples belong to the class of noncommutative spaces (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
          %p
            Mathematically better defined are notions (sometimes equated by various authors with the class of quantum groups) like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitriangular+Hopf+algebras"} quasitriangular Hopf algebras
            %span.newWikiWord
              quantum matrix groups
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/quantum+matrix+groups"} ?
            (quantum linear groups, more general FRT-algebras and Majid’s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(R)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Yang-Baxter+matrix"} quantum Yang-Baxter equation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+categories"} braided monoidal categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
          %p
            There is an extensive geometric theory of homogeneous spaces for quantum groups and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            whose structure groups are quantum groups.
